<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Bangash Nawabs of Farrukhabad</h1><p class="st-meta">1714 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Farrukhabad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Afghan principality in the Ganges Doab founded by Muhammad Khan Bangash, a soldier of fortune in the late Mughal Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-khan-bangash">Muhammad Khan Bangash</a>, an Afghan commander who served the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhsiyar">Farrukhsiyar</a>, was granted lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-yamuna-doab">Doab</a> and founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhabad">Farrukhabad</a> in 1714, naming it after the emperor. He later served as Mughal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, and built a hereditary principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Bangash nawabs ruled a territory between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> populated by Afghan settlers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> farmers. They fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/awadh-state">Awadh State</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-khan-bangash">Ahmad Khan Bangash</a> defeated an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> army in 1750 but was then crushed by a Maratha and Awadh alliance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhabad">Farrukhabad</a> became a centre of textile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 1770s the nawabs depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, and in 1802 Nawab Imdad Husain Khan ceded his territory to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">British East India Company</a> in exchange for a pension. The last nawab joined the 1857 rebellion and was exiled.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img